----
id: top_theme_1
title: Hold AI companies liable for the harm they cause
----
- Enact criminal liability for harm caused by AI
- Direct more resources for real enforcement
----
id: top_theme_1_quotes
translator_note: "Quotes as edited by the writer (9/23). region and field values are data keys; do not translate them."
session: 10
quote_1: "With social media, we didn’t hold companies accountable, and it tore down our democracy. I think that the era of saying that companies aren't responsible for how people use tools like this should end. I bet they would change policy and invest in certain tools to manage better if we ended that lack of accountability."
attribution_1: Arts, entertainment and media worker from the Bay Area
region_1: Bay Area
field_1: "Arts, entertainment, or media"
quote_2: "To your point, Section 230 of the CDA made it so that a media company would be actively punished if they tried to police user content, rather than making it something that would align their values to the public good. So, if we're looking at incentive structures holistically: where can we structure the rules so that following the rules works towards the public good and to the bottom line, rather than creating tension between them?"
attribution_2: Information technology worker from the Central Coast
region_2: Central Coast
field_2: "Information technology"
quote_3: "One thing could be who's accountable for the outputs. If there's a human evaluation, then the human is accountable, because a human should be the final decision maker. But the way AI is being used, if human evaluation is not involved, then who's accountable for the mistakes that AI is making?"
attribution_3: Finance worker from the Bay Area
region_3: Bay Area
field_3: "Financial"
----
----
id: top_theme_2
title: Protect people and nature from the resource needs of AI
----
- Study the impact of data centers on the environment
- Set limits on data center noise, pollution, and use of water and electricity
----
id: top_theme_2_quotes
translator_note: "Quotes as edited by the writer (9/23). region and field values are data keys; do not translate them."
quote_1: "I think we should protect our natural resources, and a clean environment. Because I know people in Texas where data farms or data centers have moved in, and the water has been polluted, and electric bills go way up."
attribution_1: Arts/entertainment/media worker from Southern California
region_1: Southern California
field_1: "Arts, entertainment, or media"
quote_2: "My biggest concern is the amount of water and electricity AI is using. You hear of the data center in Georgia that illegally tapped into some city’s or county's water supply and took millions of gallons from it without anybody realizing."
attribution_2: Government worker from LA
region_2: Los Angeles
field_2: "Government"
quote_3: "When I think about the state as a physical place, we could set environmental regulations, we could set data privacy requirements for states that operate here. I get really excited about what floor we are setting for environmental protection when it comes to energy use, space use, etc."
attribution_3: Government worker from the Bay Area
region_3: Bay Area
field_3: "Government"
----
----
id: top_theme_3
title: Set rules for where AI can be used safely
----
- Limit using AI to make decisions that impact lives
- Ban automated AI in the hiring process
----
id: top_theme_3_quotes
translator_note: "Quotes as edited by the writer (9/23). region and field values are data keys; do not translate them."
quote_1: "When it comes to employment, it should be a human decision. We should not let technology do that for us."
attribution_1: Manufacturing worker from the Bay Area
region_1: Bay Area
field_1: "Goods producing and harvesting"
quote_2: "The criteria that is used by AI is often a black box. For example, it could be biased towards screening resumes that are younger candidates over older candidates."
attribution_2: Science worker from the Bay Area
region_2: Bay Area
field_2: "Professional services"
quote_3: "Maybe a requirement to release an AI model to the public requires it to pass some bias testing?"
attribution_3: Manufacturing worker from the Bay Area
region_3: Bay Area
field_3: "Goods producing and harvesting"
----
----
id: tier_1
title: Other ideas with strong support
subtitle: ""
icon: heart
nbr_ideas: 23
----
----
id: tier_1_idea_1
title: Make rules about how AI handles personal data
----
- Set rules for gathering, handling, securing, and deleting
- Enforce transparency and privacy protections
----
id: tier_1_idea_2
title: Set rules around how AI works
----
- Incent companies to align with human standards
- Set guardrails for AI overuse and addiction
----
id: tier_1_idea_3
title: Deter layoffs because of AI, reward firms that keep or add workers
----
- Add penalties for firing workers for AI
- Create incentives to keep or increase jobs
----
id: tier_1_idea_4
title: Make AI policymaking more open to the public
----
- Give affected communities their say
- Create citizen peer groups that include tech people
----
id: tier_1_idea_5
title: Make companies reskill or give career help to workers who lose their jobs to AI
----
- Incent them to retrain workers instead of laying them off
- Shift workers to underserved areas
----
id: tier_1_idea_6
title: Set rules for labeling AI use
----
- Mark AI content with common words or watermarks
- Penalize companies when they don’t disclose AI use
----
id: tier_1_idea_7
title: Restrict and tax AI companies’ profits
----
- Close loopholes that let them avoid liability
- Change tax laws so stock can’t be used to avoid taxation
----
id: tier_1_idea_8
title: Hold AI companies liable if they use the work of others without permission
----
- Enforce copyrights, defend IP
- Companies pay for training data into creator fund
----
id: tier_1_idea_9
title: Make people aware of how to use AI ethically
----
- Make public service ads on benefits and risks
- Guide residents about risks posed by AI, to privacy, children, etc
----
id: tier_1_idea_10
title: Improve AI education in schools
----
- Let students work in sandboxes to scale positive effects
- Data literacy taught in k-12 schools
----
id: tier_1_idea_11
title: Allow people to opt out of AI and make that easier
----
- Require consent, make opt-out the default
- Let people opt out of their data being used
----
id: tier_1_idea_12
title: Support independent research on AI's effects
----
- Fund studies on how AI use affects critical thinking
- We need more evidence on the harm and risks. From there we would be able to define a strategy for how we would approach regulating people and their interaction with chatbots. Then we should provide that to people to make the decision on whether to engage further.
----
id: tier_1_idea_13
title: Limit what personal data AI systems can collect, and let users manage their data
----
- Make consent text simple
- Limit collection of data in public settings (so it can’t even get into AI)
----
id: tier_1_idea_14
title: Pay workers who lose jobs due to AI while they find new jobs
----
- Universal basic income
- More unemployment pay if you lost your job to AI
----
id: tier_1_idea_15
title: Support AI training for workers
----
- Offer free AI training for state employees and expanded out to the public
- Research programs to figure out how best to retrain workers
----
id: tier_1_idea_16
title: Use AI to improve how government works
----
- Legislation to utilize AI with human oversight to root out waste and fraud within the public sector to make sure the money is going to the right people
- Government should license new AI models
----
id: tier_1_idea_17
title: Keep humans in the loop as decision-makers, don't rely solely on AI
----
- Preserve role of human decision makers
- Establish guidelines for human evaluation in relevant use cases and encourage accountability to related processes
----
id: tier_1_idea_18
title: Strengthen parental controls for AI use
----
- Oversight on how technology companies interact with / provide influence / devices / tech to schoolkids. Require more restrictive, easier to find, easier to use parental controls/administrator guardrails on tech companies that make tools children can access. Ensure kids get AI/tech education in school, including critical thinking about what information they see online.
- Introduce stronger protections for children, like having informed consent from parents and firewalls against using AI chatbots. Include warnings of harm from AI usage for children. Link child and parent online accounts for supervision.
----
id: tier_1_idea_19
title: Fund programs for human connection and life outside of AI
----
- Go back to in-person learning and testing
- Fund third-spaces and arts/sports programs to promote community health
- Fully fund high quality knowledge and information services (such as education, libraries, mental healthcare)
----
id: tier_1_idea_20
title: Make sure students don't use AI to avoid learning
----
- Make sure students disclose using AI in schoolwork.
- Check the usage of AI by students
- Prohibit AI from most school work
----
id: tier_1_idea_21
title: Keep companies from firing or punishing workers for not using AI
----
- Require employers to include employees in decisions to introduce AI tools, either for employee use or decisions that affect employees
- Companies can’t fire people who won’t use AI tools
----
id: tier_1_idea_22
title: Track and report on job loss due to AI
----
- Transparent, continuous reporting on AI’s effect on jobs lost and economy
----
id: tier_1_idea_23
title: Use AI for jobs people don’t want to do or can’t do
----
- Align AI use to places where it’s socially beneficial
- Have AI do jobs people can’t do or don’t want to do
----
id: how_to_read_tiers
----
### How to read this ranking

The ranking shows how an idea fared across discussions. It's not a verdict on its merit. An idea that’s not in the top may still matter a great deal to many people.

These are the themes participants talked about. They are separate from what current policy or legislation may already do. Some may align with items already moving through the policy landscape.
