----
id: what_happens_next
----
## What happens next

This report is not the end of the story. The ideas people shared will join a fast-moving policy conversation already underway.

As Engaged California finalized its findings, the Newsom administration acted on AI. Many actions touched on themes that people raised in these discussions:

- The environmental footprint of data centers
- Transparency about AI-generated content
- Independent oversight of frontier models
- Protections for workers and children

In September 2026, Governor Newsom signed:

- One of the nation's broadest [data center laws](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/21/governor-newsom-signs-most-comprehensive-data-center-laws-in-the-nation-providing-communities-more-control-on-water-electricity-and-land-use/). This gives communities more say over use of their water, electricity, and land.
- Senate Bill 1050, which [requires disclosure](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/16/governor-newsom-signs-new-law-to-protect-workers-require-disclosures-on-ai-generated-advertising/) when an ad uses AI-generated performers.
- An Executive Order to speed up the state’s [AI oversight](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/18/governor-newsom-issues-executive-order-to-accelerate-independent-oversight-and-advance-the-creation-of-an-ai-kill-switch/) efforts. This fast-tracks new AI auditing laws and advances a "kill switch" for frontier AI models.
- A set of [first-in-the-nation AI safeguards](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/09/governor-newsom-signs-first-in-the-nation-ai-safeguards-to-protect-californians-calls-on-the-federal-government-to-do-its-part/).
- [Child safety laws](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/10/governor-newsom-signs-the-strongest-child-safety-chatbot-and-social-media-laws-in-the-nation/) for chatbots and social media.

We're not done with our work either. Californians raised many ideas that will inform government action in the years ahead. And as the AI landscape keeps evolving, we will keep listening.
